const code = `use std::sync::Arc;
use stateknot::agent_http::{AgentHttpOptions, AgentHttpService};

// service: AgentServiceV1 with exact deployments and resource policy.
// verifier: your real AgentHttpAuthenticator, not a test token check.
let options = AgentHttpOptions::new(["agents.example.com".to_owned()])?;
let http = AgentHttpService::new(service, Arc::new(verifier), options);
let router = http.router();
// Mount behind TLS. Run Workers separately. Own and drain the listener.`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Submit and recover Agents over authenticated HTTP."
  description="Four bounded JSON operations over the durable Agent service, with verified identity, exact retries and two-phase cancellation."
  section="Agent HTTP v1"
  locale="en"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="http-boundary"
  >
    <h2 id="http-boundary">Implemented JSON profile, not a stable release</h2>
    <p>
      <code>AgentHttpService</code> adds a real HTTP router over <a
        href="/docs/agent-service/">AgentService v1</a
      >. It durably admits work but never executes a model, Tool or graph in the
      request task. StateKnot remains pre-alpha; SSE and a complete managed
      hosting service are separate work.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-wiring">
    <h2 id="http-wiring">Bind verified identity and resource policy</h2>
    <p>
      A mandatory <code>AgentHttpAuthenticator</code> validates signature or introspection
      evidence, issuer, audience, expiry and revocation. It maps a trusted tenant/principal
      and independent <code>Submit</code>, <code>Read</code> and <code
        >Cancel</code
      > grants. There is no default verifier or anonymous mode.
    </p>
    <p>
      The existing <code>AgentServiceAuthorizer</code> still authorizes the exact
      Agent/request, Run or key digest before resource lookup. Never trust identity
      fields in JSON, an unverified JWT or arbitrary forwarding headers. Client budgets
      can only narrow host policy.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · host wiring</span><button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="http-code"
          ><span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span></button
        >
      </div>
      <pre id="http-code" tabindex="0"><code>{code}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-operations">
    <h2 id="http-operations">Four operations, one durable service</h2>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs</dt><dd>
          Serialize <code>AgentHttpSubmission</code>: submission_key, exact
          versioned agent identity, and schema-bound request. 201 creates a Run;
          200 recovers it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>GET /v1/agent-runs/&#123;run_id&#125;</dt><dd>
          No request body. 200 returns an integrity-verified public snapshot,
          not private checkpoints or transcript.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs/lookup</dt><dd>
          Serialize <code>AgentHttpLookup</code> with submission_key. Keep the key
          out of the URL and access logs.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>POST /v1/agent-runs/&#123;run_id&#125;/cancellation</dt><dd>
          Serialize caller-retained <code>AgentCancellationIds</code> with event_id
          and failure_id. 202 records the request; 200 recovers the exact pair.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Use a Bearer credential and JSON POST bodies. Accept must be absent, <code
        >application/json</code
      > or <code>*/*</code>. Rust wire types expose JSON Schema; unknown fields,
      duplicate keys (including nested keys), query strings and noncanonical
      paths are rejected.
    </p>
    <p>
      Success returns <code
        >AgentHttpRunResponse &#123; request_id, snapshot &#125;</code
      >. A fresh request_id correlates this response; it is not an idempotency
      key. <code>Active</code> does not prove a Worker is executing and may include
      failure closure. <code>CancellationRequested</code> is not terminal cleanup.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-retry">
    <h2 id="http-retry">Recover a lost response without creating more work</h2>
    <p>
      Persist a high-entropy submission key and exact logical request before
      sending. Persist both cancellation IDs before cancelling. On timeout,
      disconnect, shutdown or an oversized response, the database may already
      have committed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read by the original key/Run or retry the same content and identities with
      bounded backoff. Changed submission content or a competing cancellation
      pair is 409. Inspect durable state; do not generate a new key merely
      because the response was lost. A recovered snapshot may have a newer
      revision.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-limits">
    <h2 id="http-limits">Finite limits and payload-safe errors</h2>
    <p>
      Defaults: 256 KiB request, 2 MiB response, 64 concurrent requests per
      shared instance and a 15-second cooperative deadline. Hard ceilings: 2 MiB
      request, 8 MiB response, 1,024 concurrent requests and 60 seconds. JSON
      also has structural bounds. Blocking host code cannot be preempted by an
      async timeout.
    </p>
    <p>
      400 rejects malformed input; 401/403 reject credential or policy; 404
      follows authorization; 405/406/415 reject unsupported HTTP forms; 409
      reports conflict; 413 rejects body size/read failures; 429 signals
      capacity; 503 signals unavailable/deadline/shutdown; 500 signals
      integrity/configuration or response limits.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every response is private/no-store JSON with a correlation ID and
      API-version header. Errors expose only a fixed <code>agent_http.*</code> code
      and request_id, never raw token, SQL or provider messages. 429 and 503 include
      <code>Retry-After: 1</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="http-operations-checklist">
    <h2 id="http-operations-checklist">
      Before exposing a production endpoint
    </h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Pin compatible source, lockfile, migrations, graphs and schemas. Run an
        independently configured scheduler/Worker; submission alone does not
        advance a Run.
      </li>
      <li>
        Configure TLS and an exact Host allowlist, connection/header limits,
        per-tenant quotas and replica-wide admission control. Forwarded headers
        do not expand trust. Origin is denied by default; optional exact HTTPS
        origins do not enable CORS.
      </li>
      <li>
        Install real credential verification and exact resource policy. Exercise
        expiry, revocation, wrong audience and tenant isolation. Never deploy
        the tests’ static credentials.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep authorization, keys, input/output and raw paths out of logs.
        Monitor bounded latency/error metrics and dependency readiness.
      </li>
      <li>
        Stop accepting connections, call <code>http.shutdown()</code>, and bound
        listener drain separately. Recover possibly committed requests with
        original identities after replacement.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Qualification covers actual HTTP + PostgreSQL 16/17, 24 concurrent
      identical submissions, lost submit/cancel responses, no inline dispatch,
      hostile input, authorization before an unavailable database, slow
      requests, overload and shutdown. It does not qualify a live issuer, live
      model, SSE service or an entire production deployment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/agent-http.md"
        >Full wire and operations guide</a
      > · <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0008-agent-http-v1.md"
        >RFC-0008</a
      > · <a href="/docs/status/">Development status</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
